<div class="prose max-w-prose">

One of my key responsibilities at Buzuci was helping run two new product
offerings as the business expanded into custom textile design. The first was a
course-based armchair textile printing offering, where participants built their
own armchairs from scratch and customised the textile designs for their pieces.

The process initially relied on an inefficient template the company already had
in place. I attended the course, worked through the existing system in real
conditions, and used that firsthand experience to identify where the process was
breaking down and where it could be simplified.

## Improving the process

I created new working templates that were easier to use, more consistent, and
far more efficient for the team. These templates were refined over time as the
course evolved, making it easier to communicate design requirements, reduce
errors, and speed up production.

This was especially important because each week involved designing and printing
multiple custom armchair textiles. The work required a deep understanding of
how artwork needed to be adapted for the chair shapes, print constraints, and
materials involved. I worked through custom design requests, adjusted artwork
where needed, and made sure the final output translated well onto the finished
product.

## Working with custom artwork and clients

A large part of this work involved communicating directly with course attendees
through phone calls, WhatsApp messages, emails, and in-person consultations.
People often arrived with ideas that needed to be interpreted, refined, and
prepared for production in a way that could actually work on the textile and on
the final furniture piece.

I also helped manage collaborations with external artists whose work was being
printed onto armchairs. In these situations, the goal was not only to make the
artwork print beautifully, but also to present it in a way that respected the
artist’s original work and satisfied their expectations as well.

## Expanding into textile collections

Alongside the armchair offering, I also prototyped, patterned, templated, and
brought out a new line of printed textile designs for dresses and skirts. These
pieces were developed for a range of materials and garment uses, including
beach cover-ups, tank tops, and other seasonal textile applications.

This involved exploring how shape, print placement, and fabric type worked
 together to create practical yet visually compelling designs. It was a product
 development role as much as a design role — translating ideas into working
 templates and repeatable design systems that could support ongoing production.

## The result

This work helped create a more efficient, scalable design and production
process for custom textile printing and expanded the product offering beyond its
original format. It brought together design thinking, manufacturing awareness,
and client communication in a hands-on way, while helping turn one-off ideas
into repeatable product lines.

